{
  "$schema": "https://docs.oasis-open.org/sarif/sarif/v2.1.0/errata01/os/schemas/sarif-schema-2.1.0.json",
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "deadset-go",
          "version": "1.6.0",
          "semanticVersion": "1.6.0",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "DS1001",
              "name": "unused-exported",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An exported symbol with no reference in the target and no reference from any loaded consumer."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An exported symbol with no reference in the target and no reference from any loaded consumer. A symbol referenced only from its own declaration site counts as unreferenced."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An exported symbol with no reference in the target and no reference from any loaded consumer. A symbol referenced only from its own declaration site counts as unreferenced."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "problem": {
                  "severity": "error"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "DS1002",
              "name": "unused-unexported",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An unexported symbol with no reference in the target."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An unexported symbol with no reference in the target. A symbol referenced only from its own declaration site counts as unreferenced. In TypeScript a name an import declaration binds, or an `import x = require()` declaration that is not exported, is such a symbol of its file, reported at the binding when nothing in the file reads it: a use in a type position, in a local export or in a type, a link or a `@see` tag of a doc comment reads it, and so does JSX the compiler turns into calls of a factory it names. A binding of a declaration file or of a component file is not judged, nor one of a generated file while generated files are excluded. In Go a constant or a type a function body declares, a function literal's body included, that nothing in that body names outside its own specification is reported under this code, under the reference of the enclosing declaration, as `grammar/symbol-ref.md` states for a declaration inside a function body, at the local declaration's own position, with `symbol.kind` `constant` or `type` and the liveness relation `reference-counting`."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An unexported symbol with no reference in the target. A symbol referenced only from its own declaration site counts as unreferenced. In TypeScript a name an import declaration binds, or an `import x = require()` declaration that is not exported, is such a symbol of its file, reported at the binding when nothing in the file reads it: a use in a type position, in a local export or in a type, a link or a `@see` tag of a doc comment reads it, and so does JSX the compiler turns into calls of a factory it names. A binding of a declaration file or of a component file is not judged, nor one of a generated file while generated files are excluded. In Go a constant or a type a function body declares, a function literal's body included, that nothing in that body names outside its own specification is reported under this code, under the reference of the enclosing declaration, as `grammar/symbol-ref.md` states for a declaration inside a function body, at the local declaration's own position, with `symbol.kind` `constant` or `type` and the liveness relation `reference-counting`."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "problem": {
                  "severity": "error"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "DS1003",
              "name": "unused-member",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A struct field, class member or type member with no reference, a private member included."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A struct field, class member or type member with no reference, a private member included. A member referenced only from its own declaration site counts as unreferenced."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A struct field, class member or type member with no reference, a private member included. A member referenced only from its own declaration site counts as unreferenced.\n\nA TypeScript member that no value writes and nothing reads, whose type names a type parameter of its declaring interface, class or type alias, is not reported when every part of that declaration that names the type parameter is a member no value writes and nothing reads. TypeScript compares object types by their members, so such members are what make two instantiations of their type distinct types, and deleting them leaves the type parameter phantom and lets one instantiation stand for another. A member a value writes is decided by the rule of DS1301 instead, whatever its type names."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "problem": {
                  "severity": "error"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "DS1004",
              "name": "test-only-use",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A symbol with zero production references and at least one test reference: an unused-exported, unused-unexported or unused-member candidate whose test reference count is not zero, reported once under this code."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A symbol with zero production references and at least one test reference: an unused-exported, unused-unexported or unused-member candidate whose test reference count is not zero, reported once under this code. A reference from a consumer's test files is a test reference unless the configuration counts consumer tests as production. A reference from test-support code, a package or file that only tests reach and that carries test evidence of its own language, is a test reference. A declaration of test-support code is test code and is no subject of this code, as grammar/analysis.md states. An interface method whose only calls and selections through the interface are in test code, and that DS1203's precondition does not exempt, is reported under this code. The methods implementing it are not reported with it, because the test reaches them through it. A candidate found dead under reachability, every production reference to which is made by a declaration itself dead, is reported under this code as well where test code that is no test of dead code, and that the run counting test references reaches, references it, because that test keeps it, and the finding's message names both kinds of reference."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A symbol with zero production references and at least one test reference: an unused-exported, unused-unexported or unused-member candidate whose test reference count is not zero, reported once under this code. A reference from a consumer's test files is a test reference unless the configuration counts consumer tests as production. A reference from test-support code, a package or file that only tests reach and that carries test evidence of its own language, is a test reference. A declaration of test-support code is test code and is no subject of this code, as grammar/analysis.md states. An interface method whose only calls and selections through the interface are in test code, and that DS1203's precondition does not exempt, is reported under this code. The methods implementing it are not reported with it, because the test reaches them through it. A candidate found dead under reachability, every production reference to which is made by a declaration itself dead, is reported under this code as well where test code that is no test of dead code, and that the run counting test references reaches, references it, because that test keeps it, and the finding's message names both kinds of reference."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "problem": {
                  "severity": "error"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "DS1005",
              "name": "test-of-dead-code",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A test symbol whose set of referenced target symbols is non-empty and every member of that set is reported dead."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A test symbol whose set of referenced target symbols is non-empty and every member of that set is reported dead. In TypeScript an ambient declaration, one a declaration file holds or one a `declare` modifier carries or a declaration carrying one holds, describes code the target does not implement, so it is no target symbol of that set. A test that references at least one live target symbol is never reported, no notion of a test's subject and no name matching enters the rule, and the message states the rule. The test joins the dead component of the symbols it references, and it and each of them count as referencing each other, so the test is a member of their cycle and never a dead symbol outside it."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A test symbol whose set of referenced target symbols is non-empty and every member of that set is reported dead. In TypeScript an ambient declaration, one a declaration file holds or one a `declare` modifier carries or a declaration carrying one holds, describes code the target does not implement, so it is no target symbol of that set. A test that references at least one live target symbol is never reported, no notion of a test's subject and no name matching enters the rule, and the message states the rule. The test joins the dead component of the symbols it references, and it and each of them count as referencing each other, so the test is a member of their cycle and never a dead symbol outside it."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "problem": {
                  "severity": "error"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "DS1006",
              "name": "deprecated-unused",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A symbol carrying a deprecation marker and no production reference: an unused-exported, unused-unexported or unused-member candidate whose symbol is deprecated, reported once under this code."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A symbol carrying a deprecation marker and no production reference: an unused-exported, unused-unexported or unused-member candidate whose symbol is deprecated, reported once under this code. Go identifies the marker by the convention the standard tools recognize, TypeScript by the documentation tag its tools recognize."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A symbol carrying a deprecation marker and no production reference: an unused-exported, unused-unexported or unused-member candidate whose symbol is deprecated, reported once under this code. Go identifies the marker by the convention the standard tools recognize, TypeScript by the documentation tag its tools recognize."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "problem": {
                  "severity": "error"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "DS1101",
              "name": "unnecessary-export",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An exported symbol whose every reference is inside the symbol's own package or module, reported as a candidate for unexporting."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An exported symbol whose every reference is inside the symbol's own package or module, reported as a candidate for unexporting. The subject is a package-level declaration or a method. Three subjects are excluded. An interface method is excluded because its exportedness is the contract of the interface that declares it. A struct field is excluded because an encoder reads it by name. A method that satisfies an interface some symbol uses as a type is excluded because it cannot be unexported without its type ceasing to satisfy that interface, and so is a method whose name and signature are those of a method of an exported interface type declared by a package outside the target that the import closure holds, because that interface fixes them whether or not the program converts the method's type to it. The finding names the narrower visibility the references support. A declared cross-language edge counts as an out-of-package reference. Where the edge's other side is unknown to the analyzer, the finding is emitted pending. A reference from a Go external test package, a test file whose package clause names its directory's package with the suffix _test, is a reference from outside the package, because that test builds as another package and needs the export."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An exported symbol whose every reference is inside the symbol's own package or module, reported as a candidate for unexporting. The subject is a package-level declaration or a method. Three subjects are excluded. An interface method is excluded because its exportedness is the contract of the interface that declares it. A struct field is excluded because an encoder reads it by name. A method that satisfies an interface some symbol uses as a type is excluded because it cannot be unexported without its type ceasing to satisfy that interface, and so is a method whose name and signature are those of a method of an exported interface type declared by a package outside the target that the import closure holds, because that interface fixes them whether or not the program converts the method's type to it. The finding names the narrower visibility the references support. A declared cross-language edge counts as an out-of-package reference. Where the edge's other side is unknown to the analyzer, the finding is emitted pending. A reference from a Go external test package, a test file whose package clause names its directory's package with the suffix _test, is a reference from outside the package, because that test builds as another package and needs the export.\n\nClosed world only. A Go main package, and an internal/ directory tree for importers outside its parent, are closed worlds whatever the configuration declares, because no other program can import them, so this finding is reported there always. A published package is a closed world only when the configuration declares the consumer set complete and every declared consumer loads. A library with no consumer loaded and no complete consumer set declared gets this finding on its internal/ tree and its main packages and never on its published API. A symbol the rule of DS1301 holds for is not reported under this code, whatever severity the configuration gives DS1301, because the write-only finding names the defect to fix first. A type that a parameter or a result of an exported function or method of its own package names is not reported when a reference from outside the package references that function or method, because the caller holds values of the type, and unexporting it would leave an exported signature naming a type its callers cannot name. A package-level declaration whose name a string literal spells carries the class grammar/analysis.md gives it."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "problem": {
                  "severity": "warning"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "DS1102",
              "name": "unnecessary-exposure",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An exported symbol of a non-internal package whose every reference is inside the target module, reported as a candidate for relocation behind an internal boundary."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An exported symbol of a non-internal package whose every reference is inside the target module, reported as a candidate for relocation behind an internal boundary. The subject is a package-level declaration or a method. Three subjects are excluded. An interface method is excluded because its exportedness is the contract of the interface that declares it. A struct field is excluded because an encoder reads it by name. A method that satisfies an interface some symbol uses as a type is excluded because it cannot be unexported without its type ceasing to satisfy that interface, and so is a method whose name and signature are those of a method of an exported interface type declared by a package outside the target that the import closure holds, because that interface fixes them whether or not the program converts the method's type to it. The finding names the narrower visibility the references support. A declared cross-language edge counts as an out-of-package reference. Where the edge's other side is unknown to the analyzer, the finding is emitted pending. A reference from a Go external test package, a test file whose package clause names its directory's package with the suffix _test, is a reference from outside the package, because that test builds as another package and needs the export."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An exported symbol of a non-internal package whose every reference is inside the target module, reported as a candidate for relocation behind an internal boundary. The subject is a package-level declaration or a method. Three subjects are excluded. An interface method is excluded because its exportedness is the contract of the interface that declares it. A struct field is excluded because an encoder reads it by name. A method that satisfies an interface some symbol uses as a type is excluded because it cannot be unexported without its type ceasing to satisfy that interface, and so is a method whose name and signature are those of a method of an exported interface type declared by a package outside the target that the import closure holds, because that interface fixes them whether or not the program converts the method's type to it. The finding names the narrower visibility the references support. A declared cross-language edge counts as an out-of-package reference. Where the edge's other side is unknown to the analyzer, the finding is emitted pending. A reference from a Go external test package, a test file whose package clause names its directory's package with the suffix _test, is a reference from outside the package, because that test builds as another package and needs the export.\n\nClosed world only. Reported always for a main package and an internal/ directory tree, and for a published package only when the configuration declares the consumer set complete and every declared consumer loads. A library with no consumer loaded and no complete consumer set declared gets this finding on its internal/ tree and its main packages and never on its published API. A symbol the rule of DS1301 holds for is not reported under this code, whatever severity the configuration gives DS1301, because the write-only finding names the defect to fix first. A type that a parameter or a result of an exported function or method of its own package names is not reported when a reference from outside the target module references that function or method, because the caller holds values of the type, and moving it behind an internal boundary would leave an exported signature naming a type its callers cannot name."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "problem": {
                  "severity": "warning"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "DS1103",
              "name": "unreachable-export",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An unused exported symbol in a package or file no external code can import: an unused-exported candidate whose enclosing package or file is unimportable from outside, reported once under this code at the certain class, because unimportability is a property of the package graph rather than of the consumer set, except where the confidence rule of grammar/analysis.md gives a declaration a string literal spells the class possible."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An unused exported symbol in a package or file no external code can import: an unused-exported candidate whose enclosing package or file is unimportable from outside, reported once under this code at the certain class, because unimportability is a property of the package graph rather than of the consumer set, except where the confidence rule of grammar/analysis.md gives a declaration a string literal spells the class possible."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An unused exported symbol in a package or file no external code can import: an unused-exported candidate whose enclosing package or file is unimportable from outside, reported once under this code at the certain class, because unimportability is a property of the package graph rather than of the consumer set, except where the confidence rule of grammar/analysis.md gives a declaration a string literal spells the class possible."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "problem": {
                  "severity": "error"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "DS1201",
              "name": "unused-interface",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An interface no symbol uses as a type, counting uses from every loaded module."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An interface no symbol uses as a type, counting uses from every loaded module. The finding names the concrete implementations and their positions, and the interface's members join its dead component rather than being reported as independent unused declarations."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An interface no symbol uses as a type, counting uses from every loaded module. The finding names the concrete implementations and their positions, and the interface's members join its dead component rather than being reported as independent unused declarations."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "problem": {
                  "severity": "error"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "DS1203",
              "name": "uncalled-interface-method",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An interface method that no call site invokes or selects through the interface, whatever the number of implementations."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An interface method that no call site invokes or selects through the interface, whatever the number of implementations. A call or selection in test code is no call for this rule, and an interface method only test code calls is DS1004's. The finding names the concrete implementations and their positions. Each method that implements it and that nothing else keeps is reported under the code its own references select. Such a method counts as referenced by the interface method alone, so it belongs to this finding's dead component, whose root member is the interface method."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An interface method that no call site invokes or selects through the interface, whatever the number of implementations. A call or selection in test code is no call for this rule, and an interface method only test code calls is DS1004's. The finding names the concrete implementations and their positions. Each method that implements it and that nothing else keeps is reported under the code its own references select. Such a method counts as referenced by the interface method alone, so it belongs to this finding's dead component, whose root member is the interface method.\n\nIn Go, every method of an exported interface whose method set holds an unexported method is exempt, a method of an interface it embeds included. An unexported interface restricts no implementor outside its package, so a method only it holds is judged. That is the sum-type shape, whose method set exists to restrict the implementors, and a TypeScript interface cannot take it because it declares no member less visible than itself. In both languages, every marker method is exempt, an interface method whose every implementation carries an empty body."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "problem": {
                  "severity": "warning"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "DS1204",
              "name": "unused-satisfaction-assertion",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A compile-time satisfaction assertion whose interface no symbol uses as a type."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A compile-time satisfaction assertion whose interface no symbol uses as a type."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A compile-time satisfaction assertion whose interface no symbol uses as a type.\n\nIn a library, an assertion whose interface and whose concrete type are both published API, each an exported declaration of a package outside programs can import, is not reported: it states to every importer a promise of the published API. An assertion whose interface alone is published API has the reachability class possible, whatever consumers the run declares and loads. The assertion checks a promise the published API makes, that the type satisfies the published interface, and that promise is made to every program that imports the package, not only to the consumers the run declares. A loaded consumer that names the interface as a type uses it, and the assertion is not reported."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "problem": {
                  "severity": "error"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "DS1301",
              "name": "write-only-symbol",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A package-level or module-level variable, struct field, class member, type member or collection that production code writes and never reads."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A package-level or module-level variable, struct field, class member, type member or collection that production code writes and never reads. In production mode a read from a test file counts as no read. A write from a loaded consumer's code counts as a read, because the deletion the finding names would break a consumer the target does not own. A write in a consumer's test file counts as a read in the run that counts test references. It counts in a production sweep only where the configuration counts consumer tests as production. In Go a read counts only from a declaration that is live under the finding's liveness relation, which is reference counting: a read from a declaration nothing references, whose own finding has the reachability class `certain`, is no read, so a declaration that only such declarations read is reported where a declaration something references writes it. A comparison of struct values reads every field of the type: in Go, a value of a struct type used as an operand of `==` or `!=`, as a map key, or as a `switch` tag or `case` expression is a read of every field of that type, transitively through the fields of every struct type it holds, recorded at the comparison, because equality reads every field and a field that decides equality carries information. In Go a type argument the program instantiates a type parameter with, where the parameter's constraint is `comparable` or embeds it, is compared as an operand of `==` of that type is, recorded at the instantiation, so the struct values `slices.Equal`, `slices.Index` or `maps.Equal` compares have every field read. `reflect.DeepEqual` is not a comparison under this rule. In Go, a conversion the compiler accepts between two struct types, or between two pointer types that are not named types and whose base types are struct types, reads every field of the two struct types where their struct type literals are distinct, recorded at the conversion, because the conversion is legal only while their fields are identical, ignoring tags, and so no field of either can be deleted alone. It reads in the same way every field of each pair of distinct struct type literals the identity of the two types compares in turn: through each field's type, and from there through the element, key, base, parameter and result types of the type literals it reaches, through the method signatures of an interface type literal, and through the type arguments of an instantiated generic type. A field reached there has no reference of its own, as grammar/symbol-ref.md states, so it is never a subject and those reads hold no finding back. It stops at a defined type, whose fields the conversion does not read, because a defined type is identical to itself whatever fields it declares. A pair whose two sides are one struct type literal, the two struct types themselves included, as where one type is declared from the other or the conversion is to the operand's own type, reads nothing, because a field deleted from that literal leaves both sides identical. Where the operand's type or the conversion's type is a type parameter, the struct type its constraint's core type is takes that side's place, and a type parameter operand reads in the same way the fields of each struct type argument an instantiation the program holds gives it, an instantiation made through another generic declaration's own type parameters included. A conversion to any other type, an interface type among them, reads no field under this rule. In TypeScript a comparison of two object references reads no member, because `===` compares identity and never a member. A TypeScript parameter property, a constructor parameter that carries a modifier, declares a member of its class that the constructor writes with its argument, so the member is judged under this rule, and under every rule that reads a class member, as a member the class body declares is. In TypeScript a property of an object literal writes the member of the same name that the literal's contextual type declares, whether or not the compiler checks the literal for excess properties. Where that contextual type is a union or an intersection, the property writes the member of that name on each constituent that declares one. So a literal that a declaration annotates, a function returns, a call passes or a `satisfies` expression checks writes the members of the type it is given. An object literal handed to a callee the target does not implement has its members read, as grammar/analysis.md states. A store through the value a field or a member holds reads that field or member rather than writing it, because the store lands in storage every other holder of the value reads. In Go that store is an assignment, an increment, a compound assignment or a `delete` whose target is reached through a struct field holding a map, a slice or a pointer. Examples are `s.f[k] = v`, `s.f.x = v` and `delete(s.f, k)`. The same store through a field holding an array or a struct is a store into the field itself. TypeScript holds every object by reference, so an element or property store through a member's value reads the member. The finding names each write position, so the deletion set is visible. Deleting the subject and its writes also deletes every declaration whose only uses they hold. Such a declaration is a type parameter of the subject's declaring type that the subject's type alone names, an import binding the writes alone use, or a declaration whose every use is in the type annotation of the subject or of another subject this rule reports, or in the writes those findings name. The finding's message names each such declaration, and no finding is reported about the declaration itself."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A package-level or module-level variable, struct field, class member, type member or collection that production code writes and never reads. In production mode a read from a test file counts as no read. A write from a loaded consumer's code counts as a read, because the deletion the finding names would break a consumer the target does not own. A write in a consumer's test file counts as a read in the run that counts test references. It counts in a production sweep only where the configuration counts consumer tests as production. In Go a read counts only from a declaration that is live under the finding's liveness relation, which is reference counting: a read from a declaration nothing references, whose own finding has the reachability class `certain`, is no read, so a declaration that only such declarations read is reported where a declaration something references writes it. A comparison of struct values reads every field of the type: in Go, a value of a struct type used as an operand of `==` or `!=`, as a map key, or as a `switch` tag or `case` expression is a read of every field of that type, transitively through the fields of every struct type it holds, recorded at the comparison, because equality reads every field and a field that decides equality carries information. In Go a type argument the program instantiates a type parameter with, where the parameter's constraint is `comparable` or embeds it, is compared as an operand of `==` of that type is, recorded at the instantiation, so the struct values `slices.Equal`, `slices.Index` or `maps.Equal` compares have every field read. `reflect.DeepEqual` is not a comparison under this rule. In Go, a conversion the compiler accepts between two struct types, or between two pointer types that are not named types and whose base types are struct types, reads every field of the two struct types where their struct type literals are distinct, recorded at the conversion, because the conversion is legal only while their fields are identical, ignoring tags, and so no field of either can be deleted alone. It reads in the same way every field of each pair of distinct struct type literals the identity of the two types compares in turn: through each field's type, and from there through the element, key, base, parameter and result types of the type literals it reaches, through the method signatures of an interface type literal, and through the type arguments of an instantiated generic type. A field reached there has no reference of its own, as grammar/symbol-ref.md states, so it is never a subject and those reads hold no finding back. It stops at a defined type, whose fields the conversion does not read, because a defined type is identical to itself whatever fields it declares. A pair whose two sides are one struct type literal, the two struct types themselves included, as where one type is declared from the other or the conversion is to the operand's own type, reads nothing, because a field deleted from that literal leaves both sides identical. Where the operand's type or the conversion's type is a type parameter, the struct type its constraint's core type is takes that side's place, and a type parameter operand reads in the same way the fields of each struct type argument an instantiation the program holds gives it, an instantiation made through another generic declaration's own type parameters included. A conversion to any other type, an interface type among them, reads no field under this rule. In TypeScript a comparison of two object references reads no member, because `===` compares identity and never a member. A TypeScript parameter property, a constructor parameter that carries a modifier, declares a member of its class that the constructor writes with its argument, so the member is judged under this rule, and under every rule that reads a class member, as a member the class body declares is. In TypeScript a property of an object literal writes the member of the same name that the literal's contextual type declares, whether or not the compiler checks the literal for excess properties. Where that contextual type is a union or an intersection, the property writes the member of that name on each constituent that declares one. So a literal that a declaration annotates, a function returns, a call passes or a `satisfies` expression checks writes the members of the type it is given. An object literal handed to a callee the target does not implement has its members read, as grammar/analysis.md states. A store through the value a field or a member holds reads that field or member rather than writing it, because the store lands in storage every other holder of the value reads. In Go that store is an assignment, an increment, a compound assignment or a `delete` whose target is reached through a struct field holding a map, a slice or a pointer. Examples are `s.f[k] = v`, `s.f.x = v` and `delete(s.f, k)`. The same store through a field holding an array or a struct is a store into the field itself. TypeScript holds every object by reference, so an element or property store through a member's value reads the member. The finding names each write position, so the deletion set is visible. Deleting the subject and its writes also deletes every declaration whose only uses they hold. Such a declaration is a type parameter of the subject's declaring type that the subject's type alone names, an import binding the writes alone use, or a declaration whose every use is in the type annotation of the subject or of another subject this rule reports, or in the writes those findings name. The finding's message names each such declaration, and no finding is reported about the declaration itself."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "problem": {
                  "severity": "error"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "DS1302",
              "name": "unused-enum-member",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An enumerated member that no symbol names, at the reachability class derived for the member and with no confidence ceiling below it."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An enumerated member that no symbol names, at the reachability class derived for the member and with no confidence ceiling below it. Every member of a type that carries a string, text or binary conversion method, or that a conversion from an integer or a wire value produces, is retained by the enum-group exemption first, so a member reached only by value is never a candidate."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An enumerated member that no symbol names, at the reachability class derived for the member and with no confidence ceiling below it. Every member of a type that carries a string, text or binary conversion method, or that a conversion from an integer or a wire value produces, is retained by the enum-group exemption first, so a member reached only by value is never a candidate."
              },
