            {
              "id": "DS1303",
              "name": "unused-type-parameter",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A type parameter of a function or method that no part of the declaration's signature and no part of the declaration's body names, at the certain class."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A type parameter of a function or method that no part of the declaration's signature and no part of the declaration's body names, at the certain class. The deletion is local to the declaration and the explicit instantiations the reference set already lists. A type parameter of a function or method that is itself dead falls with it and is reported under no code of its own."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A type parameter of a function or method that no part of the declaration's signature and no part of the declaration's body names, at the certain class. The deletion is local to the declaration and the explicit instantiations the reference set already lists. A type parameter of a function or method that is itself dead falls with it and is reported under no code of its own.\n\nFunction and method type parameters only. A type parameter of a type declaration is never reported, because a phantom type parameter such as `type ID[T any] int` makes two instantiations distinct types while naming the parameter nowhere, so deleting it changes the program. A method signature an interface or an object type declares belongs to that type declaration, so its type parameters are never reported either."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "problem": {
                  "severity": "error"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "DS1501",
              "name": "file-never-built",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A source file no configuration in the build matrix builds."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A source file no configuration in the build matrix builds. On the Go side the finding names the build constraint that excluded the file. A file whose build constraint holds only when the ignore tag is set is never reported, because grammar/analysis.md keeps such a file outside the analysis wherever no configuration builds it: the tag is the toolchain's convention for a file kept out of ordinary package builds unless a configuration explicitly satisfies the tag, and built by hand, the toolchain applying no build constraint to a file named on its own command line. A file any other custom tag excludes is reported under a matrix the configuration declares complete, because completeness is the maintainer's assertion that the listed configurations are every one the target builds, and a configuration the maintainer builds by hand belongs in that list."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A source file no configuration in the build matrix builds. On the Go side the finding names the build constraint that excluded the file. A file whose build constraint holds only when the ignore tag is set is never reported, because grammar/analysis.md keeps such a file outside the analysis wherever no configuration builds it: the tag is the toolchain's convention for a file kept out of ordinary package builds unless a configuration explicitly satisfies the tag, and built by hand, the toolchain applying no build constraint to a file named on its own command line. A file any other custom tag excludes is reported under a matrix the configuration declares complete, because completeness is the maintainer's assertion that the listed configurations are every one the target builds, and a configuration the maintainer builds by hand belongs in that list.\n\nReported only when the configuration declares the build matrix complete, and never for a file the toolchain ignored solely because it imports \"C\" under a build with cgo disabled. Such a file is recorded as excluded by cgo rather than as never built."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "problem": {
                  "severity": "error"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "DS1502",
              "name": "file-never-imported",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A source file that no import from live code reaches, that no root names, and in which no declared cross-language edge names a declaration: an import counts where it is written in a file a root, a live declaration or such an import keeps, or in a live declaration, so a file only dead code imports is reported, under reachability."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A source file that no import from live code reaches, that no root names, and in which no declared cross-language edge names a declaration: an import counts where it is written in a file a root, a live declaration or such an import keeps, or in a live declaration, so a file only dead code imports is reported, under reachability. An import written in a loaded consumer reaches a file of the target as an import written in the target does. An edge's side names a declaration as a root names one, so a file holding a declaration an edge names is evaluated with the edge rather than reported: whether that declaration is dead is the edge evaluation's to say, and the merge resolves it against the paired side."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A source file that no import from live code reaches, that no root names, and in which no declared cross-language edge names a declaration: an import counts where it is written in a file a root, a live declaration or such an import keeps, or in a live declaration, so a file only dead code imports is reported, under reachability. An import written in a loaded consumer reaches a file of the target as an import written in the target does. An edge's side names a declaration as a root names one, so a file holding a declaration an edge names is evaluated with the edge rather than reported: whether that declaration is dead is the edge evaluation's to say, and the merge resolves it against the paired side."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "problem": {
                  "severity": "error"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "DS1601",
              "name": "unused-dependency",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A directly declared dependency that no import in the target needs."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A directly declared dependency that no import in the target needs. On the Go side, a direct require whose module provides no package any target package or test variant imports. On the TypeScript side, a manifest dependency, development dependency or peer dependency the project's import closure does not need. A deletion finding whose fix would remove the last use of a dependency names that dependency. On the TypeScript side, a needed dependency whose every use is written inside declarations that are members of the run's dead components, or at the top level of a file reported under DS1502, that no command runs and that no needed dependency used otherwise names as a peer, is reported as well, dead only through that code: at the reachability class possible where one of those declarations belongs to a library's published API, and found under reachability alone where one of those declarations or files is, so at probable unless ts.roots_complete is true. A use the configuration makes, a use an unanswered specifier may make and an import none of whose bindings is used are uses by live code."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A directly declared dependency that no import in the target needs. On the Go side, a direct require whose module provides no package any target package or test variant imports. On the TypeScript side, a manifest dependency, development dependency or peer dependency the project's import closure does not need. A deletion finding whose fix would remove the last use of a dependency names that dependency. On the TypeScript side, a needed dependency whose every use is written inside declarations that are members of the run's dead components, or at the top level of a file reported under DS1502, that no command runs and that no needed dependency used otherwise names as a peer, is reported as well, dead only through that code: at the reachability class possible where one of those declarations belongs to a library's published API, and found under reachability alone where one of those declarations or files is, so at probable unless ts.roots_complete is true. A use the configuration makes, a use an unanswered specifier may make and an import none of whose bindings is used are uses by live code.\n\nOn the Go side the rule is the semantics of `go mod tidy -diff` exactly: a requirement marked indirect is never reported, because it exists to pin a transitive version and removing it changes the build list. On the TypeScript side the import closure is the files of the run's projects, and a call of `import.meta.resolve` or of `require.resolve` in them, whatever `require` names, whose first argument is a string literal or a template literal with no substitution needs each dependency an import of that specifier needs. A `@types/<name>` package in a type root of a compiler configuration that lists no `types`, a `node_modules/@types` directory at or above the configuration's directory or a `typeRoots` entry naming one, is needed by a use, in a file of that configuration, of a global name or an ambient module that one of the package's declaration files declares, which resolves to no declaration of the program: a compiler before version 6 includes every package of the type roots where `types` is not listed, and version 6 and later include none, so the program the analysis builds holds none of those declarations. A package's declaration files are the file its `types` or `typings` names, else its `index.d.ts`, and each file a `/// <reference path>` of one of them reaches inside the package. A global is a top-level declaration of such a file that is no module, or a declaration of a `declare global` block of one that is, and an ambient module is a `declare module` with a string name of a file that is no module. A name that names a member, a property or a label is no use of a global. Four more dependencies are needed: one whose command a script entry, a workflow step or a git hook runs, one that a string of a configuration file names, and one that a string names under a manifest key or a package key of an applied convention row, each as grammar/analysis.md states, and one that a needed dependency names as a peer, as stated below. A command no script entry, workflow step or git hook runs needs nothing. A declared dependency that the installed manifest of a needed declared dependency names in its peerDependencies is needed, and so on until no further dependency joins. A peer dependency is a package the needed dependency expects beside it at a version its manifest states, whether or not it imports the package, and the project's declaration is what keeps that package present at a version the project chooses, so removing the declaration can break the needed dependency where nothing the analysis reads imports the peer. A peer that peerDependenciesMeta marks optional is needed in the same way, because the needed dependency's manifest still names it as a package it works with, and a package manager may leave an optional peer out where the project does not declare it, so the project's declaration is what keeps it present for the needed dependency. A declared dependency that only dependencies nothing needs name as a peer is needed by nothing. A dependency of a manifest whose directory holds source only in configurations the run lists in configurations_not_built is reported at the reachability class possible, because the source that may import it was not analyzed. An installed manifest is the package's manifest in the nearest node_modules directory at or above the directory of the manifest that declares the dependency, and a dependency with none is decided by the import closure alone. On the TypeScript side, the dependencies judged are those of every manifest grammar/analysis.md says the analysis reads, a nested package's among them, each read against its own directory: a dependency is needed by a use written in a file at or below the directory of the manifest that declares it where no manifest between them declares a dependency of the same name, so a use below a nested manifest that declares the same dependency needs that nested manifest's declaration only, by a use no one file holds, and as a peer of a needed dependency of the same manifest or of a manifest below it. A dependency a nested package's manifest declares whose installed manifest the analysis cannot find is not judged, because no run requires a nested package to be installed and its commands and peers cannot be read, while a workspace member's and the target's own are judged as above."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "problem": {
                  "severity": "error"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "DS1605",
              "name": "unused-module-directive",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A replace directive whose target module is absent from the build list, the one case in which the directive is exactly a no-op."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A replace directive whose target module is absent from the build list, the one case in which the directive is exactly a no-op."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A replace directive whose target module is absent from the build list, the one case in which the directive is exactly a no-op.\n\nOnly a replace whose target is absent from the build list. No other module-file directive is reported. An exclude directive is not, because not selected is a counterfactual about a resolution that did not happen. A workspace use entry and a tool directive are not, because each is an external entry point whose invocation lives outside anything the analysis reads."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
