                }
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "DS1807",
              "name": "dead-store",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A write to a local variable with no read before the next write to it or the end of its scope, computed on the same read-and-write classification the write-only-symbol kind uses."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A write to a local variable with no read before the next write to it or the end of its scope, computed on the same read-and-write classification the write-only-symbol kind uses. The finding names the write position."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A write to a local variable with no read before the next write to it or the end of its scope, computed on the same read-and-write classification the write-only-symbol kind uses. The finding names the write position.\n\nIn TypeScript the binding of a catch clause is a local variable the clause writes when it catches, so a binding that is a name and that the clause's block never reads is a dead store, positioned at the binding: the clause written with no binding behaves the same. A TypeScript local is a name a `let` or `const` declaration of a block binds, or a `var` declaration of a block of a function, a name an object or array binding pattern of one binds at any depth included, except a name with a default, whose removal skips the default's evaluation, and a name beside a rest element of its object pattern, whose removal changes what the rest holds. A `var` of a block nested in its function's body is judged in that block, its last write kept because the function scope reads on past the block's end, and is no subject where a nested function of its function names it. A local a nested function reads is no subject, because the function may read it at any time, and every write to a local nothing reads is dead, one a nested function makes included, each positioned at the name it writes. The variable a `let` or `const` declaration of a `for…of` or `for…in` statement binds is written at each iteration, so one the loop body never reads is a dead store positioned at the binding, except a name that starts with an underscore, because a loop must bind a name and that spelling declares it unread. In Go a function literal a package-level variable's initializer holds is a function body of its own, and a finding about one of its stores carries the reference of the variable the literal initializes, which for a blank variable is its package's, as `grammar/symbol-ref.md` states for a blank-identifier declaration. In Go a range statement stores its key and its value at the start of every iteration, and an empty range stores neither. In Go a function literal reads each variable it names where it is written, and a store that a literal naming its variable may precede on some path of the control-flow graph, the literal of an earlier iteration of a loop included, is not dead, because a call of the literal the graph does not order may read it. A store no such literal precedes is judged as any other. A literal that is the callee of the call it is written in, outside a `go` or `defer` statement, runs there, so it reads its variables at that call and precedes no later store."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "problem": {
                  "severity": "error"
                }
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "DS1809",
              "name": "unreachable-case",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A switch case whose type or value an earlier case in the same switch already covers."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A switch case whose type or value an earlier case in the same switch already covers."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A switch case whose type or value an earlier case in the same switch already covers."
